<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alash (party)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1917">1917</a>–<a href="/years/1920">1920</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Alash Party</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alash was a Kazakh national party founded in 1917 by intellectuals including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alikhan-bukeikhanov">Alikhan Bukeikhanov</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhmet-baitursynov">Akhmet Baitursynov</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirzhakip-dulatov">Mirzhakip Dulatov</a>, and named after the legendary ancestor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhs">Kazakhs</a>. It sought Kazakh autonomy within a democratic Russian federation and formed the Alash Orda government, which lasted from December 1917 until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> dissolved it in 1920. Many of its leaders were later killed in Stalin's purges.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alash-party.jpg" alt="Alash (party)" width="320" height="291" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Товарищество Азамат · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qazaq_newspaper_logo.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Alash grew out of the weakening of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/february-revolution">February Revolution</a>, which Kazakh elites used to found a party of their own. An all-Kazakh congress held in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orenburg">Orenburg</a> from 21 to 28 July 1917 decided that Russia should become a federal parliamentary republic. The creation of the party was announced in the weekly newspaper Qazaq. Its founders, among them its chairman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alikhan-bukeikhanov">Alikhan Bukeikhanov</a>, had been members of the liberal Constitutional Democratic Party, with which they kept some relations, so the party counts among the successors of the Constitutional Democrats.</p><p>The word Alash means tribesman or relative in the Turkic languages, and some sources treat it as a synonym for Kazakh; the chronicler Qadyrgali Jalaiyr often used it in that sense. The party chose it in the hope of uniting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkic-peoples">Turkic peoples</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> under one identity rather than making the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhs">Kazakhs</a> a titular nation, a plan that did not succeed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Alash called for equal treatment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhs">Kazakhs</a> and Russians and an end to Russian settlement on Kazakh lands, and it was the first modern organised political group of the Kazakh and Kyrgyz elite. It won elections in most regions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a>, which in effect founded the Alash Autonomy, with Bukeikhanov as its president. Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secularism">secularism</a> and ties to the Muslim world divided its intelligentsia through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-civil-war">Russian Civil War</a>. The party ceased to exist on 26 August 1920, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> defeated the White forces holding the territory and formed the Kirghiz Autonomous Socialist Soviet Republic.</p><p>Some former members then joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-party-of-the-soviet-union">Communist Party of the Soviet Union</a> and translated school textbooks. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakh-famine-of-1930-1933">Asharshylyk</a> famine Kazakh elites protested against propaganda and the censorship of news of the famine, in the letter of five of 4 July 1932 and in a letter from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turar-ryskulov">Turar Ryskulov</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a>. In the 1930s most of them were arrested in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-purge">Great Purge</a> and sent to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulag">Gulag</a>, and between 1935 and 1938 most of the Kazakh elite was shot.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a> became independent, many figures linked to the party were rehabilitated and monuments were raised to them. On 4 July 2021 a monument to the movement's leaders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhmet-baitursynov">Akhmet Baitursynov</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alikhan-bukeikhanov">Alikhan Bukeikhanov</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirzhakip-dulatov">Mirzhakip Dulatov</a> was unveiled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astana">Astana</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alash (party)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Alash</b> party was founded by Kazakh intellectuals including Alikhan Bukeikhanov, Akhmet Baitursynov and Mirzhakip Dulatov.</blockquote><a href="/state/alash-autonomy">Alash Autonomy</a></li></ul></section>
